{"title":"Zsigmondy's Theorem","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><p><b>Zsigmondy's Theorem</b> states that, for positive <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Relatively_prime\" title=\"Relatively prime\">relatively prime</a> integers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/d/c7d457e388298246adb06c587bccd419ea67f7e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/3/8136a7ef6a03334a7246df9097e5bcc31ba33fd2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"14\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/8/2/082852571ecc48ed136ba13dcffa8f6e40064bbd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a&gt;b$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"43\" height=\"15\" />, there exists a prime number <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/f/36f73fc1312ee0349b3f3a0f3bd9eb5504339011.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> (called a <i>primitive prime factor</i>) such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/3/9/e39ea1cbfbce419e14fcf946c601370056fff23f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p|(a^n-b^n)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"88\" height=\"20\" /> but <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/a/3/7a37d0945879fc42fc3d9fef22edf5a725bfb0af.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p\\nmid(a^k-b^k)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"96\" height=\"22\" /> for all positive integers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/4/0/7400e866ec206aeeca3f416d787b8bbeed9e1b54.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k&lt;n$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"45\" height=\"13\" /> EXCEPT (i) if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/a/9/5a9c444294c130c4ef37c39cbccab2e057d3657e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n=a-b=1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"110\" height=\"16\" />, (ii) if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/b/6cb7ae00764b56ee2adf59e78a1ffde9685b80db.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n=2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"46\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/d/add2609af1bb7632cc22b68fd49fdd94de06b327.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a+b$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"41\" height=\"16\" /> is a power of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, or (iii) if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/f/2/2f2a6991d2108f237ee451bf37db91cacaf4134e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n=6$\" width=\"43\" height=\"12\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/5/a/55afdab16c8c49089c4d9ceeb8d3c8cf1f088be6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a=2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"45\" height=\"14\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/f/5/cf590e9abb33aa9776ddc386b1ba6ee7b43fa413.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b=1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"40\" height=\"13\" />. This theorem can sometimes be used to prove that no more solutions exist to <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Diophantine_equations\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"Diophantine equations\">Diophantine equations</a>.\n</p>\n<div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Example\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Example</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Proof\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Proof</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Problems\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problems</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Example\">Example</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Zsigmondy%27s_Theorem&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Example\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We desire to find all solutions <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/5/3/e53b1fe25be1c679117fb44a6a886fe1247d189a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(x,y)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"44\" height=\"20\" /> to the Diophantine <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/9/b19879fc5a1ce8475359e3315cdff274fb3eed12.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3^x-1=5^y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"92\" height=\"16\" />. We notice that the first integer <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> for which <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/0/7/f07bbf5e0176739bfb300666a92dc6b147933290.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5|3^x-1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"65\" height=\"20\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/e/7/2e7d65cfa316d639c9267398009812f3eb45fea9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x=4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"46\" height=\"14\" />, which produces <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/d/f/9df1511fdbc00bb6e0d248d407722f5958feb243.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3^4-1=80\\neq 5^y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"133\" height=\"20\" />. Now, by Zsigmondy's Theorem (the exceptions do not apply here), for any positive integers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/b/6/cb6055493807459fe9b3d658e9683a25d1cd63bf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n\\ge 4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"44\" height=\"14\" />, there must exist a primitive prime factor for each of the terms <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/5/b/c5bb12740d3a9d6be7de605fb81620d24f9816de.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3^n-1^n.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"65\" height=\"16\" /> However, the right hand side of the equation only contains prime factors of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/0/79069377f91364c2f87a64e5f9f562a091c8a6c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, and so there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/1/1/711a34fab65b1f8dd88da6ce3ef8f45c3a67d4ee.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\text{no solutions}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"100\" height=\"25\" /> to this Diophantine equation.\n</p><p><i>Note: We could, alternatively, observe the left hand side is always even while the right hand side is always odd. This produces a much faster contradiction. ~Mathkiddie</i>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Proof\">Proof</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Zsigmondy%27s_Theorem&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Proof\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://www.awesomemath.org/wp-pdf-files/math-reflections/mr-2025-02/mr_2_2025_zsigmondys_theorem.pdf\">https://www.awesomemath.org/wp-pdf-files/math-reflections/mr-2025-02/mr_2_2025_zsigmondys_theorem.pdf</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problems\">Problems</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Zsigmondy%27s_Theorem&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Problems\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<ul><li>Find all solutions <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/3/c73ed093cc73a8d9a14185fa525e7067e5c3725d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(x,y,z)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"58\" height=\"18\" /> to the Diophantine equation <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/2/79280a7cda4ab31fb3aa0025f193006433df69a0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$7^x-1=2\\cdot3^y\\cdot5^z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"144\" height=\"16\" />.</li>\n<li>Prove that the equation <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/c/f/4cfb6bfeed7142bd42239e8f131971cf9c4f094f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(x+y)^n=x^m+y^m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"159\" height=\"20\" /> has a unique solution in integers with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/7/6/8763b276b45bcaa9d42bb9c7638fdd70404e7f55.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x&gt;y&gt;0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"77\" height=\"16\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/3/f/53fd131f75abaaae86368e81b15ed0d2a517dd41.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m,n&gt;1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"67\" height=\"15\" />.</li></ul>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Zsigmondy%27s_Theorem&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<ul><li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Diophantine_equations\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"Diophantine equations\">Diophantine equations</a></li></ul>\n<p><i>This article is a stub.  Help us out by <span class=\"plainlinks\"><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php?title=Zsigmondy%27s_Theorem&amp;action=edit\">expanding it</a></span>.</i>\n</p>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261017155324\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: [show‐toc]\nCPU time usage: 0.019 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.031 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 170/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 306/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 0/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 4/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 5287/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    5.690      1 Template:Stub\n100.00%    5.690      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:31171:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261017155324 and revision id 289312. Rendering was triggered because: page-view\n -->\n</div>"}